    "technical_and_design_decisions": [
        "Approach: Replace internal copies of Fabric code with direct dependencies on externalized Fabric Go modules/packages.",
        "Module Structure: Assume Hyperledger Fabric has externalized these components as Go modules (e.g., github.com/hyperledger/fabric-protos, github.com/hyperledger/fabric/common) that can be imported directly.",
        "Backward Compatibility: Assume externalized packages maintain API compatibility with the internal copies to minimize SDK interface changes.",
        "Temporary Workaround (U6): Implement a simple check to honor go.mod files in chaincode packages for backward compatibility during transition.",
        "Testing Strategy: Focus on integration tests to ensure SDK functionality remains unchanged after dependency replacement.",
        "Build Tooling: Use Go modules dependency management throughout."
    ],
    "assumptions": [
        "All referenced Fabric components are available as stable external Go packages/modules with the same APIs as the internal copies.",
        "The development environment has proper access to Hyperledger Fabric repositories and Go module infrastructure.",
        "No major refactoring of existing SDK interfaces is required - only import path changes.",
        "The temporary workaround (U6) is a simple, low-risk implementation that can be done quickly.",
        "Testing can leverage existing test suites without requiring new test infrastructure.",
        "No coordination with other teams is required for package publication - they already exist.",
        "Estimated effort includes code analysis, implementation, testing, and basic documentation updates.",
        "The developer is familiar with the Fabric Go SDK codebase and Go module system."
